.TH SIMPLE_SHELL 1 "September 2026" "hsh 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
.br
.B hsh
[command_file]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simplified clone of the shell. It reads commands, finds them either
directly (a path containing a
.B /
) or by searching the
.B PATH
environment variable, and executes them in a child process.
.PP
When standard input is a terminal,
.B hsh
runs in interactive mode: it prints a
.B ($)
prompt before reading each line. When standard input is not a terminal
(for example, when it is piped or redirected from a file),
.B hsh
runs in non-interactive mode and prints no prompt, matching the behavior
of
.BR sh (1).
.SH BUILTIN COMMANDS
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell. The shell's exit status is that of the last command run.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment, one variable per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hsh
exits with the exit status of the last command it executed. If a command
is not found, the exit status is 127.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run interactively:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ ./hsh
($) /bin/ls
($) exit
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run a single command non-interactively:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run a script of commands:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ ./hsh < commands_file
.RE
.fi
